<h2>Indian mango for export: Alphonso and Kesar</h2>
<p>Mango is one of India's best-known fresh-produce exports, and Alphonso — grown along Maharashtra's Konkan coast in Ratnagiri and Devgad — is the variety most international buyers mean when they ask for Indian mango. Kesar, from Gujarat's Junagadh region, is the other major premium export variety, prized for its aromatic sweetness.</p>
<p>A note on the trade data below: the HS code (0804.50) also captures guavas and mangosteens alongside mango, so the total slightly overstates mango alone — treat it as directionally representative rather than a mango-only figure.</p>

<h2>Two different buyer conversations: GCC volume vs UK/US/German value</h2>
<p>The UAE and Saudi Arabia are India's largest mango markets by both value and volume — a higher-volume, more price-sensitive trade, often shipped by sea or shorter air routes. The UK, Germany and US trade looks very different: smaller volumes, but a materially higher price per kilogram, reflecting premium air-freighted fruit aimed at buyers who prioritise Alphonso/Kesar quality and are prepared to pay for it. If you're comparing a quote against something you've seen elsewhere, make sure you know which of these two markets that reference price was for — they aren't the same product conversation even though the fruit is nominally the same.</p>

<h2>Mango is seasonal — plan around it</h2>
<p>Unlike many of our other product lines, mango has a hard seasonal window: roughly April through July, tracking Maharashtra and Gujarat's harvest. Outside that window, genuine Alphonso/Kesar simply isn't available from current-season Indian stock. See our <a href="/resources/harvest-calendar/">harvest calendar</a> for how this compares with our other seasonal produce, and plan your order timing accordingly — early-season enquiries get first access to peak-quality fruit.</p>

<h2>How should I specify mango?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Variety.</strong> Alphonso and Kesar are the two flagship export varieties; tell us which fits your market and budget.</li>
  <li><strong>Ripeness at shipment.</strong> Mature-green for longer sea-freight transit, colour-turning for shorter air-freight routes — this depends on your transit time, not just preference.</li>
  <li><strong>Size grade.</strong> Expressed as count-per-box (e.g. 8–10 count per 3–4kg carton) — larger fruit means a lower count per box.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pricing</h2>
<p>Send your variety, grade, quantity, destination and preferred shipping method (air or sea) and we'll quote current terms — remembering that mango pricing moves through the season as supply peaks and tapers.</p>

